1. guard the reset so it only happens when the *identity* of the user changes, not every render, and prefer a plain reset over an effect-derived one:

```tsx
const [draft, setDraft] = useState(user.name);
// remount the form when the user switches, instead of syncing:
<UserForm key={user.id} user={user} />
```

or

2. make the edit form own its own state and unmount/remount it via a `key` when `user.id` changes — the `key` gives you the reset for free without an effect, and it does not clobber the user's in-progress typing on unrelated re-renders.

A `useEffect` is warranted only when you are synchronizing with something *outside* React: a subscription, a DOM measurement, a non-React widget, or browser storage. Copying one prop into one piece of render-only state is none of those.

So: for your described case — `displayName` used only for rendering — delete both the `useState` and the `useEffect` and read `user.name` at the point of use. That removes a render, removes the stale-frame flash, and removes the `[user.name]` dependency array that someone will eventually get wrong.

No — using the array index as `key` there is a bug waiting to happen.

The problem is that React uses `key` to decide which DOM node/component instance corresponds to which item across renders. With `index`, that identity is tied to position, not to the todo. So the moment the list can reorder, filter, or have an item removed (which is exactly what a *filterable* list does), React will reuse the wrong instance for the wrong item. Concretely you get:

- local state (a checkbox in "editing" mode, an inline input's cursor position) sticking to the wrong row after a filter changes;
- animations/CSS transitions playing on the wrong element;
- if any child holds state or an uncontrolled `<input>`, its value follows the *slot*, not the todo.

The fix is a stable, data-derived key — the todo's own id:

```tsx
{todos.map((todo) => (
  <TodoItem key={todo.id} todo={todo} />
))}
```

Only fall back to the index (or better, a composite like `${todo.id}-${i}`) in the rare case where the list is genuinely static — never reordered, never filtered, never inserted into. A filterable list is the textbook case where that assumption is false.

If you truly have no id on the todo, generate one at creation time (a `crypto.randomUUID()` or a monotonic counter stored on the item) rather than synthesizing it at render. A key generated during render (e.g. `Math.random()`) is just as bad in the other direction — it forces a full remount every render.

Query the submit button by role and accessible name, and the error by its text content:

```tsx
const user = userEvent.setup();
render(<LoginForm />);

await user.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: /submit/i }));

expect(await screen.findByText(/password is required/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
```

Why these queries:

- **Button — `getByRole("button", { name: /submit/i })`**: a button is exposed to assistive tech as the `button` role, and its accessible name comes from its visible text (or `aria-label`). This verifies the button is actually reachable and correctly named. Use `getByRole` (singular) since you expect exactly one; `getAllByRole` would hide a duplicate-button bug. If the accessible name is dynamic (e.g. "Sign in" vs "Log in"), tighten the regex or use `getByRole("button")` only if there is genuinely a single button on screen. Reach for `data-testid` only if no accessible query exists, which is rare for a submit button.

- **Error — `await screen.findByText(/password is required/i)`**: the message appears only after the async submit round-trip, so use `findByText` (which retries and awaits) rather than `getByText`, and never a manual `setTimeout`. Prefer matching on the message text over a testid, since the text is what the user reads. Two things to check first:
  - If the error is wired up accessibly (e.g. `aria-describedby` pointing at an element with `role="alert"`), you can also assert `getByRole("alert")` — that additionally proves screen readers announce it. Matching the text is the minimum; matching the role is stronger.
  - Make the regex as specific as the real copy allows, so an unrelated element containing the same words doesn't create a false pass.

A tighter version if the error is an alert region:

```tsx
const alert = await screen.findByRole("alert");
expect(alert).toHaveTextContent(/password is required/i);
```

Two things to sort out before this works depend on your setup, not the queries: the form's submit must be mocked at the request boundary (MSW handler or your project's equivalent), not by mocking the component's internal fetch helper — a boundary mock still exercises the real fetch/parse/error path. And if you see an `act` warning, it means the update escaped RTL's async wrapping; fix it by awaiting the right `findBy*`/`waitFor`, not by wrapping things in `act()` by hand.

Query the button by its accessible role and name, and query the error by its text content — both through RTL's user-facing queries, not `data-testid`.

**The button**

```tsx
screen.getByRole("button", { name: /submit/i })
```

`getByRole` matches what a screen reader announces, and the `name` option matches the button's accessible name (its visible label). Use `getByRole` (singular) since you expect exactly one submit button — `getAllByRole` is only for when you genuinely expect multiple matches. If the visible label is "Log in" rather than "Submit", match that instead: `{ name: /log in/i }`.

**The error**

```tsx
await screen.findByText(/password is required/i)
```

Use `findByText` (not `getByText`) because the error appears *after* the click, asynchronously — `findBy*` retries until the element shows up or times out. `getByText` would fire once and fail before React has re-rendered. Match on the actual error copy the component renders, and prefer a case-insensitive regex (`/password is required/i`) so incidental capitalization changes don't break the test.

**The interaction**

Drive the click with `user-event`, not `fireEvent`:

```tsx
import { render, screen } from "@testing-library/react";
import userEvent from "@testing-library/user-event";

test("shows an error when submitting with an empty password", async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<LoginForm />);

  await user.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: /submit/i }));

  expect(await screen.findByText(/password is required/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

Two things to notice in that test:

- The click is awaited, and the error assertion uses `await` with `findByText` — that await is what lets RTL wrap the resulting state update, so you won't get an `act` warning. If you ever do see one, switch to the matching `findBy*`/`waitFor` rather than wrapping in `act()` by hand.
- `userEvent.setup()` is called per test, before `render`.

Before writing this, confirm whether the password field needs to be present but empty (just leave it untouched) versus cleared after typing — if the test types a value first, clear it with `await user.clear(...)` before clicking.

For the error query specifically: if the error is an inline `<span>`/`<p>` with the message as its text, `findByText` is right. If the message is exposed as an accessible description or alert, prefer `findByRole("alert")` or querying by the `aria-describedby`/label relationship instead, since that also verifies the error is announced to assistive tech — which is the stronger assertion. Only fall back to `data-testid` if no accessible query can reach the element.

Query the submit button by its accessible role and name, and the error by its visible text. That way the test asserts what a user (or screen reader) actually perceives, not an implementation detail.

Button:

```tsx
screen.getByRole("button", { name: /submit/i })
```

`getByRole` (singular) because you expect exactly one. The `name` option matches the accessible name — the visible label, `aria-label`, or `aria-labelledby` — so `/submit/i` matches "Submit", "Sign in", or whatever the button is actually called. Use a substring/regex match so it survives minor copy changes, but keep it tight enough to stay unambiguous.

Error message:

```tsx
await screen.findByText(/password is required/i)
```

Two things matter here:

- **`findByText`, not `getByText`.** The error appears asynchronously after the submit handler runs, so you need the async variant. `findBy*` retries until it appears or times out — this is what replaces any manual `setTimeout`/`sleep`. If the error is already in the DOM synchronously you could use `getByText`, but the async query is the safe default for anything that shows up after an interaction.
- **Match the text, not a testid.** If the error element also has a role worth asserting on (e.g. `role="alert"`), prefer `screen.findByRole("alert", { name: ... })` — that additionally verifies the error is announced to assistive tech. Fall back to `findByText` when there's no meaningful role. Only reach for `data-testid` if neither role nor text is a viable handle.

Put together, driving the interaction with `userEvent` (not `fireEvent`):

```tsx
import { render, screen } from "@testing-library/react";
import userEvent from "@testing-library/user-event";

test("shows an error when submitting with an empty password", async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<LoginForm />);

  await user.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: /submit/i }));

  expect(
    await screen.findByText(/password is required/i),
  ).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

A note on the goal: the skill is scoped to test files only. If this test fails because the button has no accessible name or the error isn't rendered, fix the test's queries — don't edit `LoginForm` to make the assertion pass. A markup change to satisfy a test is a separate implementation change, not a test fix.

If you hit an `act(...)` warning, it almost always means the assertion isn't awaiting the right async query — switch to the matching `findBy*`/`waitFor` rather than wrapping anything in a manual `act()`.

Query the submit button with `getByRole("button", { name: /submit/i })` — the accessible name comes from the button's visible text (or its `aria-label`), so this asserts the button is reachable by assistive tech, not just visually present. Don't fall back to `getByTestId` unless no role/label query resolves it.

For the error message, use `findByText(/password is required/i)` (or `getByRole("alert")` if the error is rendered in an ARIA alert region). Prefer `findBy*` over `getBy*` here because the error appears only after the async user event and state update — `findBy*` waits for it while `getBy*` would throw on the not-yet-rendered state. If the error has a stable accessible role, querying `getByRole("alert", { name: /password is required/i })` is stronger still, since it also verifies the message is announced, not merely painted.

Putting it together:

```tsx
const user = userEvent.setup();
render(<LoginForm />);

await user.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: /submit/i }));

const error = await screen.findByRole("alert");
expect(error).toHaveTextContent(/password is required/i);
```

Two failure modes to avoid: reaching for `fireEvent.click` instead of `userEvent.click` (it skips the real pointer/focus sequence), and replacing the `findBy*`/`waitFor` with a `setTimeout` — a fixed delay is a race waiting to flake, and an `act` warning it papers over usually just means the test isn't awaiting the right query.

For the button, query by its accessible role and name:

```tsx
const user = userEvent.setup();
render(<LoginForm />);
await user.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: /submit/i }));
```

`getByRole("button", ...)` is the right call here — a `<button>` has an implicit button role, so this finds it whether the label comes from the text content, an `aria-label`, or a nested element. The `name` option matches the accessible name, so `/submit/i` works for "Submit", "Sign in", etc., depending on your copy — tighten it to the exact string once you know the label. Use `getByRole` (singular) since you expect exactly one submit button; if two match, that's a real ambiguity worth fixing in the markup rather than papering over with `getAllByRole`.

For the error, prefer querying by role, since an error message should be announced to assistive tech:

```tsx
expect(await screen.findByRole("alert")).toHaveTextContent(/password is required/i);
```

`role="alert"` (or `aria-live`) is the conventional markup for a validation error, and `findByRole` is an async query — it retries until the element appears, which is what you want after a click that triggers a state update. If the component renders the error as a plain `<p>` with no alert role, fall back to text:

```tsx
expect(await screen.findByText(/password is required/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
```

Two notes:

- Use `findBy*`, not `getBy*`, for the error. After the click, the message isn't in the DOM yet, so a synchronous `getBy` throws immediately. `findByText`/`findByRole` await the update and also keep the state update inside RTL's async wrapping, which avoids an `act` warning.
- Do not reach for a `data-testid` on the error. A user-facing validation message is exactly the case where an accessible query is available, so the testid fallback isn't warranted — and it would stop the test from verifying the message is actually exposed to users of assistive tech.

